<section class="knowledge-hero"><nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/scriptures.html">Sacred texts</a><span>/</span><span>Mahapuranas</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">THE PURANIC LIBRARY / A REFERENCE GUIDE</p><h1>Eighteen great collections.<br><em>A library of worlds.</em></h1><p class="lead">The Mahapuranas weave cosmology, divine narratives, genealogies, sacred geography, ritual, ethics and liberation into extensive living textual traditions.</p><div class="knowledge-actions"><a class="button" href="#eighteen">Meet the eighteen</a><a href="#characteristics">What makes a Purana?</a></div></section>
<section class="section knowledge-intro" id="orientation"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / HOW TO USE THIS GUIDE</p><h2>Not eighteen<br><em>versions of one book.</em></h2></div><div><p>Each Mahapurana is a large composite work with its own textual history. Titles name traditions of transmission, not single modern volumes with identical contents everywhere. Editions, recensions and chapter arrangements can differ.</p><p>The Bhagavata Purana 12.7 gives ten characteristics of a major Purana and then names eighteen works. The same passage notes that some teachers summarise the characteristics as five.</p><div class="reader-note"><span>SOURCE TRANSPARENCY</span><p>This reference uses the owner-supplied Gita Press Bhagavata edition for the list and characteristics. Individual stories continue to identify the exact Purana and edition they follow.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section purana-characteristics" id="characteristics"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / TEN CHARACTERISTICS</p><h2>What a Mahapurana<br><em>sets in motion.</em></h2></div><p>Bhagavata Purana 12.7.9–20 expands the Puranic field beyond “old stories.” Transliteration and compact descriptions are provided as a reading map.</p></div><dl class="characteristic-grid"><div><dt>Sarga</dt><dd>Primary creation: the emergence of cosmic principles and elements.</dd></div><div><dt>Visarga</dt><dd>Secondary creation: living forms and embodied worlds unfold.</dd></div><div><dt>Vritti</dt><dd>The means by which living beings sustain life.</dd></div><div><dt>Raksha</dt><dd>Divine protection, including manifestations that restore order.</dd></div><div><dt>Manvantara</dt><dd>The administration and distinctive beings of a Manu's age.</dd></div><div><dt>Vamsha</dt><dd>Genealogies of gods, sages and royal lines.</dd></div><div><dt>Vamshanucharita</dt><dd>Accounts of figures who continue those lineages.</dd></div><div><dt>Samstha</dt><dd>Dissolution, described in several modes and scales.</dd></div><div><dt>Hetu</dt><dd>The embodied being and the causal entanglement of ignorance and action.</dd></div><div><dt>Apashraya</dt><dd>The ultimate ground that remains through changing states and names.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="section purana-directory" id="eighteen"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / THE EIGHTEEN MAHAPURANAS</p><h2>A reference<br><em>without ranking.</em></h2></div><p>The order follows Bhagavata Purana 12.7.23–24. Short notes are orientation, not summaries of every section.</p></div><ol class="purana-list">
<li id="brahma"><span>01</span><div><h3>Brahma Purana</h3><p>Cosmology, genealogies, sacred places and narratives associated with creation and pilgrimage.</p></div></li>
<li id="padma"><span>02</span><div><h3>Padma Purana</h3><p>An extensive collection spanning creation, sacred geography, devotion, pilgrimage and religious observance.</p></div></li>
<li id="vishnu"><span>03</span><div><h3>Vishnu Purana</h3><p>Creation, cosmic cycles, dynasties and Vishnu-centred theology arranged in six books.</p></div></li>
<li id="shiva"><span>04</span><div><h3>Shiva Purana</h3><p>Shaiva cosmology, worship and narratives of Shiva's household and manifestations.</p><div class="purana-story-links"><a href="/shiva-parvati.html">Shiva &amp; Parvati</a><a href="/ganesha-birth.html">Ganesha</a><a href="/kartikeya-birth.html">Kartikeya</a></div></div></li>
<li id="linga"><span>05</span><div><h3>Linga Purana</h3><p>Shaiva teachings centred on the linga, with cosmology, sacred observance and divine narratives.</p></div></li>
<li id="garuda"><span>06</span><div><h3>Garuda Purana</h3><p>A wide-ranging Vaishnava work including cosmology, worship, ethics, rites and teachings about death and afterlife.</p></div></li>
<li id="narada"><span>07</span><div><h3>Narada Purana</h3><p>Devotion, worship, sacred places and summaries that guide readers through wider sacred literature.</p></div></li>
<li id="bhagavata"><span>08</span><div><h3>Bhagavata Purana</h3><p>Twelve skandhas centred on devotion and the manifestations of Vishnu, especially Krishna.</p><div class="purana-story-links"><a href="/dhruva.html">Dhruva</a><a href="/gajendra-moksha.html">Gajendra</a><a href="/varaha-earth.html">Varaha</a><a href="/prahlada-narasimha.html">Prahlada</a><a href="/vamana-king-bali.html">Vamana</a><a href="/krishna-kaliya.html">Kaliya</a></div></div></li>
<li id="agni"><span>09</span><div><h3>Agni Purana</h3><p>An encyclopaedic collection addressing ritual, iconography, polity, literature, medicine and other fields.</p></div></li>
<li id="skanda"><span>10</span><div><h3>Skanda Purana</h3><p>A vast Shaiva corpus especially rich in sacred geography, pilgrimage traditions and regional mahatmyas.</p><div class="purana-story-links"><a href="/markandeya-shiva.html">Markandeya &amp; Shiva</a><a href="/places.html">Sacred places</a><a href="/festivals.html">Festivals</a></div></div></li>
<li id="bhavishya"><span>11</span><div><h3>Bhavishya Purana</h3><p>A layered collection whose title evokes future matters and whose received material spans ritual, observance and later historical horizons.</p></div></li>
<li id="brahmavaivarta"><span>12</span><div><h3>Brahmavaivarta Purana</h3><p>A devotional work giving major attention to Krishna, Radha, Prakriti and Ganesha.</p></div></li>
<li id="markandeya"><span>13</span><div><h3>Markandeya Purana</h3><p>Cosmology, dharma and narrative teachings, including the Devi Mahatmya's celebrated Goddess cycle.</p><div class="purana-story-links"><a href="/devi-mahishasura.html">Mahishasura</a><a href="/devi-shumbha-nishumbha.html">Shumbha–Nishumbha</a></div></div></li>
<li id="vamana"><span>14</span><div><h3>Vamana Purana</h3><p>A Puranic collection whose present form contains substantial Shaiva sacred geography alongside the name of Vamana.</p></div></li>
<li id="varaha"><span>15</span><div><h3>Varaha Purana</h3><p>Teachings voiced through the Varaha setting, with Vaishnava worship, sacred places and observances.</p></div></li>
<li id="matsya"><span>16</span><div><h3>Matsya Purana</h3><p>Flood narrative, creation, dynasties, temple and image traditions, rites and sacred geography.</p></div></li>
<li id="kurma"><span>17</span><div><h3>Kurma Purana</h3><p>Teachings framed through Kurma, integrating devotion, cosmology, yoga and accounts of sacred places.</p></div></li>
<li id="brahmanda"><span>18</span><div><h3>Brahmanda Purana</h3><p>Cosmology, cosmic geography, dynasties and the Lalita Mahatmya within its received textual tradition.</p><div class="purana-story-links"><a href="/lalita.html">Lalita Tripurasundari</a><a href="/lalita-bhandasura.html">Lalita &amp; Bhandasura</a></div></div></li>
</ol></section>
<section class="section reading-method"><p class="eyebrow">04 / READ WITH CONTEXT</p><h2>Four questions<br><em>to carry with you.</em></h2><ol><li><span>01</span><p><strong>Which Purana and which section?</strong> A title alone rarely locates a narrative precisely.</p></li><li><span>02</span><p><strong>Which edition or recension?</strong> Chapter order and details can differ.</p></li><li><span>03</span><p><strong>Who is speaking to whom?</strong> Puranic teaching is often nested inside several conversations.</p></li><li><span>04</span><p><strong>Is this text, commentary or editorial reflection?</strong> This site labels those layers separately.</p></li></ol></section>
